the load so it drifts over the smoke source, estimating a fall time of roughly 5.5 seconds from 60 m altitude, and aiming for a landing point near the origin.\n\nI'm checking the drift from the earlier test, where the load landed about 24 m west and 10 m north of where the aircraft's path would put it, since drag slowed it relative to the aircraft. I'll run a simulation to find the release point that brings it down at the origin from 60 m with a heading of 0 and a speed of 45."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "I'm planning the release point at (12.5, -29) so the drop lands at the origin, and I'll run a test drop from there at altitude 60, speed 45, heading 0."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "The fall took 0.68 s longer than the 4.96 s I predicted, so I'm going to fit an updraft to account for the gap. Next I'll compare that fitted value against the updraft figures I already have."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "Updraft 3 looks like the right fit. Test 2 is about 0.12 high and tests 3 and 4 about 0.07 high, so I'm wondering whether drag is slightly too strong. I'll try drag 0.0275 with updraft 3, and also compare updraft 3 against 3.2 to see how sensitive the result is."}, {"before_call": 14, "text": "I've got a rough wind direction of about 204 to 205 degrees, which is good enough for now. What's still unclear is how the wind veers and shears at low and high altitudes, and how an updraft behaves with different geometry through the smoke. I want to use the remaining test drops to probe more extreme conditions, so I'm planning one at altitude 150 with speed 80 on heading 270 for the long fall.\n\nI'm planning a sixth test at altitude 150 and speed 80 on heading 180 with the wind measured, and I'm checking whether a start point around (0, 400) stays within 500 m of the target."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "The last test overshot on fall time, so I'm going to ease off the drag a little and adjust the wind speed and shear. Next I'll try a few variants around those values.